<section class="phero"><div class="phero-img"><img src="/assets/img/photos/roof-shingle.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/roof-shingle-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/roof-shingle.webp 960w" sizes="100vw" width="960" height="526" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.08"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol><li><a href="/">Home</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-articles/">Guide articles</a></li><li aria-current="page">Matching and Discontinued Shingles and Siding: Why Line Items Get Disputed</li></ol></nav><p class="eyebrow on-dark">Guide · 7 min read · September 29, 2026</p><h1>Matching and Discontinued Shingles and Siding: Why Line Items Get Disputed</h1><p class="lede">When a damaged shingle or siding profile is no longer made, the question of how much has to be replaced becomes a policy- and fact-dependent dispute.</p></div></section>
<div class="wrap cols"><article class="prose"><p>A hailstorm damages one slope of a roof, or a falling limb cracks a few courses of vinyl siding on one wall. The repair seems simple until the contractor reports that the shingle or siding profile was discontinued years ago. Now the question is no longer “how many pieces were damaged” but “how much has to be replaced so the repair looks reasonable.” Matching discontinued shingles and siding is one of the most frequently disputed issues in Kentucky property claims.</p>

<h2>Why matching is disputed</h2>
<p>Insurers generally agree to pay to repair damaged property with materials of like kind and quality. Owners point out that a patch of new shingles in a different color or profile on an otherwise uniform roof is not really a repair. Insurers respond that the policy covers direct physical damage, not undamaged material that simply looks different next to the new work.</p>
<p>Both views have a basis, and the answer depends on the policy language and the facts. Kentucky does not have a statute that guarantees full matching in every claim, so you should not assume an entire roof or every elevation will be replaced just because an exact match is unavailable. Some policies address matching directly, some say nothing, and some include endorsements that limit payment for undamaged material. Your policy controls.</p>

<h2>What evidence matters</h2>
<p>Matching disputes are won or lost on evidence, not argument. The strongest files include:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Material identification.</strong> A sample shingle or siding piece, with any manufacturer markings, and a report from a matching service or supplier on whether the product is still made.</li>
<li><strong>Side-by-side comparisons.</strong> Photos of the closest available product held against the existing material in daylight.</li>
<li><strong>Repairability.</strong> For shingles, a brittleness test or documentation that old shingles tear when lifted, which can show that a repair would damage surrounding material.</li>
<li><strong>Layout.</strong> A diagram showing where the damaged area sits relative to natural breaks such as ridges, valleys, corners and trim lines.</li>
<li><strong>Visibility.</strong> Photos from the street and the main approaches showing how noticeable a mismatch would be.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Natural breaks and reasonable uniformity</h2>
<p>Even when an exact match is unavailable, a full replacement is not the only option. Appraisers and adjusters often look for a natural break, such as replacing a full elevation of siding up to an inside corner or a full roof slope up to the ridge, rather than the whole building. On the other hand, a front-facing slope with a mismatched patch at eye level from the street is a different situation from a rear slope that can only be seen from the air.</p>
<h3>Shingles</h3>
<p>Discontinued three-tab shingles are a common example. Newer architectural shingles differ in thickness, shape and exposure, so a few replacement squares can look and lay differently. The dispute often becomes whether to replace the slope, the roof or something in between.</p>
<h3>Siding</h3>
<p>Vinyl siding profiles, colors and textures change frequently, and sun fading means even a current product may not match. Aluminum and older fiber cement siding raise similar problems. Disputes often focus on how many walls should be replaced to achieve reasonable uniformity.</p>

<div class="note"><b class="h">Save samples</b>Before the roofer hauls off debris, keep a few damaged and undamaged pieces. Once the material is gone, proving what it was becomes far harder.</div>

<h2>How matching shows up in estimates</h2>
<p>In a line-item estimate, the matching question appears as quantity. The insurer’s estimate might include three squares of shingles on one slope, while the contractor’s estimate includes thirty squares for the whole roof plus tear-off, drip edge, underlayment and code items. The difference between those numbers is the dispute, and it often carries knock-on effects such as overhead and profit, steep charges and permit fees. Our <a href="/appraisal-calculators/roof-measurement-calculator/">roof measurement calculator</a> helps you estimate squares by slope so you can see the scale of each option.</p>

<h2>Questions to ask your adjuster</h2>
<p>Before a matching disagreement hardens, it helps to ask the adjuster a few specific questions in writing:</p>
<ul>
<li>Which policy language are you relying on for the matching decision?</li>
<li>Did the insurer identify a replacement product, and if so, which one and from what supplier?</li>
<li>Was a sample tested or sent to a matching service?</li>
<li>Would you consider replacing to the nearest natural break rather than the full building?</li>
</ul>
<p>The answers show whether the dispute is really about evidence, which appraisal handles well, or about the meaning of the policy, which may need a lawyer. They also give your appraiser a clear record if the claim ends up in appraisal. Keep copies of every answer, along with the dates you asked and received them.</p>

<h2>Can appraisal resolve matching?</h2>
<p>Often, yes, but with care. When a dispute is about how much replacement is needed to repair the covered damage reasonably, many appraisals treat that as part of the amount of loss. When the dispute is about whether the policy covers undamaged material at all, it may be a coverage question outside the appraisers’ authority. A common approach is for the award to separate the matching portion so the parties or a court can apply coverage afterward. If your insurer says the policy excludes matching costs, get advice from a Kentucky attorney about what that language means.</p>
<p>Our <a href="/insurance-appraisal/roof-hail-appraisal/">roof and hail appraisal</a> page explains how we approach roof disputes, and a <a href="/insurance-appraisal/second-look-estimate-review/">second-look estimate review</a> can show whether the matching gap is large enough to justify appraisal. We are independent appraisers, not attorneys or public adjusters. If you would like an honest view, <a href="/contact-us/">contact us</a>.</p><h2>Keep reading</h2><div class="rel"><a href="/appraisal-articles/flood-vs-water-damage-kentucky/"><small>Guide</small><span>Flood vs. Water Damage: Which Kentucky Losses Appraisal Can Reach</span><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a><a href="/appraisal-articles/choosing-appraiser-and-umpire/"><small>Guide</small><span>Choosing a Party Appraiser and an Umpire: Competence, Impartiality and Fees</span><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a><a href="/appraisal-articles/sinkholes-mine-subsidence-coverage/"><small>Guide</small><span>Sinkholes, Karst and Mine Subsidence: Kentucky’s Ground-Movement Coverage Gaps</span><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a></div></article>
